<!--
    branchflip: flip negated-guard if/else branches for linearity
    (angr-style `if (x)` vs the Ghidra `if (x == 0)`).

    Follow-up to PR #52 / the tee_O2 `x2nrealloc` case (`if (a0 == (void*)0x0)`
    vs the angr `if (a0)`).  The angr SAILR structurer / condition processing flips a
    branch guarded by a *negative* test (`x == 0`, `!cond`) and swaps the if/else
    arms so the common path reads top-to-bottom.  kuna does the equivalent here as
    an S8 structuring pass, `ActionBranchFlip`, gated by `option branchflip on`.

    Source (a clean diamond with a shared merge block, so it structures as a
    3-component BlockIf, the only shape that can be flipped *in place*):

        int bf(int x, int *p) {
            int r;
            if (x == 0) { p[0] = 7; r = p[1]; }
            else        { p[0] = 9; r = p[2]; }
            p[3] = r;
            return r * 5;
        }

    Compiled at gcc -O0 (no stack protector / no CET): the body is
        cmpl $0,x ; jne ELSE ; [then] ; jmp MERGE ; ELSE: [else] ; MERGE: ...
    i.e. the `==0` guard takes the THEN arm on equality.

    Default output (assert #1):  if (x == 0) { ...7... } else { ...9... }
        Ghidra/kuna keep the source polarity; the `== 0` guard renders verbatim.

    With `option branchflip on` (asserts #2/#3):
        if (x != 0) { ...9... } else { ...7... }
        ActionBranchFlip detects that the if/else guard is the negated /
        equality-to-zero form (`split_flip_in_place_test == 1`, the denormalizing
        flip class `ActionPreferComplement` leaves alone), flips the condition to
        its positive complement via the proven dual-arena machinery
        (`split_flip_in_place_execute` + `op_flip_in_place_execute` +
        `swapBlocks(1,2)`), and logs a `branchflip:` warning so the rewrite is
        observable.  The arms swap to preserve semantics.

    Stage model: this is an S8 readability-rewrites decision (`option branchflip
    on` / `off`, opt-in default-off, source_decompiler angr).  Default-off keeps
    the 675-datatest parity untouched; on requests the angr-style positive guard.

    The script decompiles the function twice in one bulk `print C` stream: pass 1
    under the default pipeline (assert #1: the issue / Ghidra polarity), pass 2
    with the option set (asserts #2/#3: the flipped positive form + the log).
-->
